  <!-- Challenge -->
 <section
    id="challenge"
    class="project-section w3-padding-64"
    aria-labelledby="challenge-heading">
    <h2 id="challenge-heading">The challenge</h2>

    <p class="w3-large">
      Students needed a way to experience the financial and strategic
      trade-offs involved in creating an employer-sponsored health plan,
      rather than learning about those trade-offs only through readings
      or lectures.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Solution -->
 <section
    id="solution"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="solution-heading">
    <h2 id="solution-heading">The solution</h2>

    <p>
      I created an interactive health insurance simulation in which students
      select a plan type and make a series of decisions about coverage and
      generosity. Each decision changes the annual premium and the estimated
      monthly employee contribution.
    </p>

    <p>
      The simulation displays these calculations throughout the experience,
      allowing students to see the financial effect of each choice immediately.
      The figures are based on real data from the University of Michigan
      Benefits Office.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- How it works -->
<section
    id="how-it-works"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="process-heading">
    <h2 id="process-heading">How it works</h2>

    <div class="w3-row-padding">

      <div class="w3-col l4 m6 s12 w3-margin-bottom">
        <div class="w3-card w3-container w3-theme-d1 w3-padding" style="height:100%;">
          <p class="w3-xxlarge"><strong>1</strong></p>
          <h3>Choose a plan</h3>
          <p>
            Students begin by selecting one of three insurance-plan types.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="w3-col l4 m6 s12 w3-margin-bottom">
        <div class="w3-card w3-container w3-theme-d1 w3-padding" style="height:100%;">
          <p class="w3-xxlarge"><strong>2</strong></p>
          <h3>Make trade-offs</h3>
          <p>
            Display logic leads each group through the appropriate path.
            Every question offers three levels of generosity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="w3-col l4 m6 s12 w3-margin-bottom">
        <div class="w3-card w3-container w3-theme-d1 w3-padding" style="height:100%;">
          <p class="w3-xxlarge"><strong>3</strong></p>
          <h3>See the costs</h3>
          <p>
            Students see the updated premium and employee contribution
            after each decision.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- My contribution -->
<section
    id="contribution"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="contribution-heading">
    <h2 id="contribution-heading">My contribution</h2>

    <ul class="w3-ul">
      <li>
        Collaborated with faculty to define the learning objectives and
        simulation structure.
      </li>
      <li>
        Organized the questions, available choices, and monetary values
        into a master decision map.
      </li>
      <li>
        Designed three branching paths using Qualtrics display logic.
      </li>
      <li>
        Developed the live premium and employee-contribution calculations.
      </li>
      <li>
        Built and tested the learner-facing interface.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Learning experience -->
<section
    id="learning-experience"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="experience-heading">
    <h2 id="experience-heading">The learning experience</h2>

    <p>
      Students work in groups of five and receive one of four fictional
      company profiles. Each profile provides information about the company
      and its employees.
    </p>

    <p>
      Acting as benefits consultants, students design an insurance plan that
      fits their assigned workforce. They must explain their choices and
      justify the trade-offs they made.
    </p>
  </section>




